# Essential UI actions must be visible (no clipped, no overlapping)

The AIC PIM admin SPA (`pim-offline-server/ui`) is the surface a
**C3PAO auditor**, an **MSP operator**, and a **government reviewer**
sit in front of. When a primary control — Rescan, Fix-It, Open,
Approve, Retest — is pushed off the right edge of a DataGrid, or when
a chip stack spills out of a fixed-height row and paints over the row
below, the page is not "slightly dense". It is **unusable** and it
fails Section 508 (WCAG 2.0 AA 1.4.10 Reflow, 2.4.7 Focus Visible,
1.4.11 Non-text Contrast) at the same time.

This happened on **Current State Compliance** (`/enclave/compliance`),
2026-07-26: per-row **Rescan** sat roughly **388 px** beyond the
viewport at 1280 px wide, Evidence and Top-categories chip stacks
overlapped neighboring rows because the grid ran at the DataGrid
default `rowHeight` of 52 px with multi-chip `Stack` cells, the page
carried a duplicate DataGrid search box, and posture / machine text
truncated with no tooltip. Nobody caught it before the operator did,
because **no screenshot and no bounding-box assertion existed** for
that page.

This rule binds the agent's UI-generation behavior AND the acceptance
evidence required before any UI change is reported as done.

## The rule

> **Every primary operator action must be visible and clickable
> without horizontal scrolling at 1280x800 and at 1024x768. Row
> content must never overlap adjacent rows or columns. No claim that
> a UI element or feature is present, available, visible, fixed, or
> working may be treated as done without **visual proof** — a
> screenshot the agent actually looked at, or a Playwright
> bounding-box assertion — captured after a full (non-`SkipUiBuild`)
> deploy. This covers **any** such claim on **any** operator surface,
> not only per-row grid actions and not only one incident page. When
> the task is visual design, operator GUI layout, DataGrid density,
> overlapping controls, or "is this page usable?", a coding pass that
> never inspects rendered output is **not** sufficient for acceptance;
> escalate **acceptance of the rendered artifact** (and **diagnosis
> after a cheaper attempt failed**) to an Opus-class model per the
> narrowed override in
> [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc). Implementation of UI
> edits stays on Grok or AUTO.**

Concretely:

1. **Primary actions are visible at 1280 and 1024.** A "primary
   action" is any control that performs the page's main verb:
   Rescan, Retest, Fix-It, Open, Approve, Deny, Reveal, Export,
   Delete. Secondary/tertiary controls may live behind an overflow
   menu; primary actions may not. The same visibility bar applies to
   any control an agent claims is present or usable — not only these
   verbs, and not only DataGrid action columns.
2. **No overlapping content.** The axis-aligned bounding box of any
   rendered row must not intersect the bounding box of any other
   row, and cell content must not paint outside its own cell.
3. **Visual proof before "done".** See the acceptance-evidence
   section below. "The build succeeded" and "the service is
   RUNNING" are **not** UI proof.
4. **Model escalation for layout work.** See the operator-record
   section below.

## Required layout patterns

### Actions column

Use exactly one of these; never neither:

| Pattern | When | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pin right** | Grid has more columns than fit at 1024 | `pinnedColumns={{ right: ['actions'] }}` and a fixed `width` on the actions column |
| **Fit the grid** | Column set can be trimmed to fit | Sum of fixed widths + `minWidth` of `flex` columns <= 1024 minus checkbox/scrollbar chrome (~80 px) |
| **Overflow menu** | More than two per-row verbs | One pinned icon button opening a `Menu`; the button itself is the primary action and must be visible |

The identity column (`display_name` or equivalent) is pinned left
together with `__check__` so the operator never loses row context
while scrolling.

### Row height and cell content

Pick one and be explicit — never rely on the DataGrid default
`rowHeight` of 52 px with variable-height cell content:

```tsx
// A. Content-sized rows (multi-chip / wrapping cells are OK here)
<ReportDataGrid
  autoHeight
  getRowHeight={() => 'auto'}
  ...
/>

// B. Fixed-height rows (cells MUST be single-line and clamped)
<ReportDataGrid
  sx={{
    '& .MuiDataGrid-cell': {
      overflow: 'hidden',
      display: 'flex',
      alignItems: 'center',
    },
  }}
  ...
/>
```

Under pattern **B**, every cell renderer clamps:

```tsx
renderCell: (params) => {
  const full = summarize(params.row)
  return (
    <Tooltip title={full}>
      <Typography
        variant="body2"
        noWrap
        component="span"
        aria-label={full}
        sx={{ maxWidth: '100%', display: 'block' }}
      >
        {full}
      </Typography>
    </Tooltip>
  )
}
```

Truncated text **always** carries the full value in a `Tooltip` and
in `aria-label`, so a screen-reader user and a mouse user both reach
the same information (Section 508 / WCAG 1.4.13).

## Forbidden constructs

In every file under `pim-offline-server/ui/src/**` and any other
operator GUI in this workspace (Win32 dialogs, Tauri shells,
`pim-ui-kit` components):

- A per-row **primary** action reachable only after horizontal
  scrolling. This is the defect this rule exists to prevent.
- A multi-chip `Stack` inside a DataGrid cell when the grid uses the
  default fixed `rowHeight` and neither `autoHeight` nor
  `getRowHeight={() => 'auto'}` is set. Chips wrap, the row does
  not grow, and the second line paints over the next row.
- A `Box sx={{ height: <fixed> }}` grid wrapper whose column widths
  sum past the wrapper width, with no pinned actions column.
- Truncated (`noWrap`, `text-overflow: ellipsis`) cell text with no
  `Tooltip` and no `aria-label` carrying the full value.
- Two search / quick-filter affordances on the same grid (page-level
  filter plus `GridToolbarQuickFilter` plus the toolbar's built-in
  search). One search per grid.
- Multi-sentence `Alert` banners stacked above a data grid on the
  page's primary working surface. Move the prose to the page intro /
  help drawer; keep the banner to one line.
- Reporting a UI change as done on the strength of `cargo build`
  output, an HTTP 200, or a `Service RUNNING` line. Those prove the
  binary shipped, not that the page is usable.
- `-SkipUiBuild` / `--features skip-ui-build` on a change set that
  touches `ui/**`, which produces "proof" screenshots of the
  **previous** bundle. See
  [`never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc`](never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc).

## Required acceptance evidence

A UI change is done when **at least one** of these exists and the
agent has actually inspected it:

### Option 1 — Playwright bounding-box assertions (preferred, durable)

```ts
const VIEWPORTS = [
  { width: 1280, height: 800 },
  { width: 1024, height: 768 },
]

for (const viewport of VIEWPORTS) {
  test(`primary actions visible at ${viewport.width}`, async ({ page }) => {
    await page.setViewportSize(viewport)
    await gotoCompliance(page)

    // 1. Primary action inside the viewport at scrollLeft 0.
    const action = page.locator('button[aria-label^="Rescan "]').first()
    const box = await action.boundingBox()
    expect(box, 'per-row Rescan must render').not.toBeNull()
    expect(box!.x).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0)
    expect(box!.x + box!.width).toBeLessThanOrEqual(viewport.width)

    // 2. No horizontal scrolling needed for the grid body.
    const overflow = await page
      .locator('.MuiDataGrid-virtualScroller')
      .first()
      .evaluate((el) => el.scrollWidth - el.clientWidth)
    expect(overflow, 'grid must not require horizontal scroll').toBeLessThanOrEqual(1)

    // 3. No row bounding boxes intersect.
    const boxes = await page.locator('.MuiDataGrid-row').evaluateAll((rows) =>
      rows.map((r) => r.getBoundingClientRect()).map((b) => ({
        top: b.top,
        bottom: b.bottom,
      })),
    )
    boxes.sort((a, b) => a.top - b.top)
    for (let i = 1; i < boxes.length; i += 1) {
      expect(
        boxes[i].top,
        `row ${i} overlaps row ${i - 1}`,
      ).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(boxes[i - 1].bottom - 1)
    }
  })
}
```

### Option 2 — screenshot the agent inspected

A full-page screenshot at 1280x800 (and 1024x768 when the change
affects column count or row height), taken against the deployed
embedded SPA, **read back by the agent** and described in the reply:
which primary action is visible, at what x-offset, and whether any
row content crosses a row boundary. A screenshot that is captured
and never looked at is not evidence.

## Operator record — model selection for visual design and GUI layout

For the record, from the workspace owner (Phil), 2026-07-26:

> **Grok 4.5 created web interfaces that were not usable** — the
> Current State Compliance DataGrid shipped with actions off-screen,
> chips overlapping neighboring rows, and dense unusable chrome —
> **and required a more expensive model (Opus) to diagnose and plan
> the fixes.**

The failure mode is not "a model wrote bad CSS". It is that a coding
pass which never renders the page **cannot know** the page is broken,
and reported the work as complete anyway. The cost lands on the
operator, who finds the defect in production and then pays for a
stronger model to do the diagnosis that should have been part of the
original acceptance step.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27) — broader than acceptance alone:

> **Use Opus for visual design.** The other models have consistently
> gotten this area of design wrong just about every time.
>
> **Preference for implementation work is Grok 4.5 Fast High
> (`cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast`) or AUTO.** Use less expensive models
> for implementation where possible.

Operator record (Phil, 2026-07-27) — why visual proof is hard:

> Visual proof requirements are a hard requirement based on previous
> experience of models saying features are available and visible, yet
> this was not true consistently — tons of missing and misaligned or
> offscreen elements.

That experience is a **general and repeated pattern**, not a one-page
DataGrid incident. The Current State Compliance Rescan / chip-stack
failure (2026-07-26) is the worked example; the requirement covers
**any** claim that a UI element is present or visible on any surface.
**A claim that a feature is available, visible, or working is not
acceptable without the visual artifact** — including affirmative claims
("I added the button and it renders"), not only layout-regression
claims ("the button is no longer clipped"). An unverified claim is
treated as not done.

### Precedence — narrow Opus carve-out for rendered acceptance (agrees with `auto-model-only`)

> **Opus survives but is narrow.** For **acceptance of rendered
> operator-GUI output** and for **diagnosis of a UI layout defect after
> a cheaper model already failed to fix it**, the Opus-class requirement
> in **this** file **overrides** the "never pin Opus" prohibition in
> [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc). Implementation of UI
> edits — including renames, navigation restructuring, adding a column,
> and wiring an export — belongs on `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO.

This is an **operator-directed precedence rule** (2026-07-27; narrowed
2026-07-28), not an agent's discretionary judgment call. When a case
falls inside the permitted set in
[`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc), an agent may use an
Opus-class model **without** the operator naming it again in the same
message — no permission-seeking, no re-confirmation each time. The two
files must agree; if they appear to conflict, follow the narrower
permitted / not-permitted lists in `auto-model-only.mdc`.

The cheaper implementer is still **fully responsible** for capturing the
proof artifact (screenshot and/or Playwright bounding-box assertion after
a full non-`SkipUiBuild` deploy). Throttling the Opus reviewer must not
become an excuse to skip visual proof — an affirmative claim that an
element is present or correct without a named artifact is still treated
as "not done".

**Do not sacrifice quality completion.** Model throttling never justifies
reducing slice scope, dropping acceptance criteria, leaving a feature
partly wired, skipping or weakening the visual-proof artifact, or
reporting work as done when verification is pending (pending must be
reported as pending, with reason and blocker named). Choosing a cheaper
model for work it will predictably get wrong, then shipping the result
unreviewed, is forbidden — an unusable cheap pass is the Opus escalation
case, not an acceptable outcome. **Ordering:** correctness and
completeness first, then cost. Cost discipline decides *which model* and
*how many passes* — never *whether* the work is finished or verified.
See the matching anti-corner-cutting clause in
[`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc).

Resolved policy both rules agree on:

| Work | Model |
| --- | --- |
| Acceptance review of rendered GUI proof; diagnosis after a cheaper UI fix failed; operator-named Opus cases | Opus-class (this override) |
| UI implementation (including mechanical edits), backend, API, ORM, lab, infrastructure, compliance, documentation, planning, exploration | `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO |

Binding consequences:

1. When the task asks **"is this page usable?"** or claims a layout
   fix is done, a coding pass that never inspects rendered output is
   **not** sufficient for acceptance, no matter how confident the
   summary sounds. Opus reviews the **rendered artifact**; it is not
   the default implementer.
2. **Implementation of UI edits** — including label/terminology
   renames, moving items between navigation menus, adding a column,
   wiring an export — uses `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or AUTO. Opus
   is for **acceptance of the rendered artifact** and for **diagnosis
   after a cheaper attempt failed** (cite the failed attempt). Under
   budget pressure that diagnosis case remains **permitted but
   bounded** — one focused pass on the specific defect (diagnosis and
   fix direction), not a general page re-review or feature rewrite —
   per [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc). Budget pressure
   removes breadth and waste, not the ability to fix a known-broken page.
3. Any reply claiming a UI element or feature is available, visible,
   fixed, or working must name its evidence artifact — the Playwright
   spec and assertion, or the screenshot path — inline. Affirmative
   claims need the same proof as regression fixes. A reply with no
   named artifact is treated as "not done". The Grok/AUTO implementer
   captures that artifact; Opus (when used) reviews it.
4. **This override does not weaken acceptance evidence.** Every
   requirement in this file stands unchanged: primary actions visible
   without horizontal scroll at 1280x800 and 1024x768, no overlapping
   row or cell content, bounding-box assertions or screenshots the
   agent actually read back, a named evidence artifact, and a full
   deploy with **no** `-SkipUiBuild` before any screenshot is treated
   as proof. The model throttle does not relax visual proof.
5. **This override does not license Opus fan-out or spend escalation
   anywhere else.** [`auto-model-only.mdc`](auto-model-only.mdc) still
   forbids Opus across a fleet of background workers, Opus retry loops
   after a usage or credit error, pinning Opus to supervise or unstick
   a stuck child, and expensive-model inheritance onto children.
   Prefer one Opus acceptance pass over Opus owning implementation
   end to end.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (Current State Compliance, as shipped 2026-07-26):

```tsx
// Fixed-height wrapper, default rowHeight 52, no pinned actions.
<Box sx={{ height: 600, width: '100%' }}>
  <ReportDataGrid
    columns={[
      { field: 'display_name', flex: 1.4, minWidth: 200 },
      { field: 'role', width: 200 },
      { field: 'evidence_kind', width: 170, renderCell: () => (
        <Stack direction="row" spacing={0.5} flexWrap="wrap">
          <Chip label={`physical_host ${phys}`} />
          <Chip label={`log_derived ${log}`} />
        </Stack>
      ) },
      { field: 'top_finding_categories', flex: 1.4, minWidth: 220, renderCell: () => (
        <Stack direction="row" spacing={0.5} flexWrap="wrap">
          {cats.slice(0, 3).map((c) => <Chip key={c.category} label={c.category} />)}
        </Stack>
      ) },
      { field: 'actions', width: 120 },
    ]}
    checkboxSelection
  />
</Box>
```

Two chips at 170 px and three chips at 220 px both wrap to a second
line inside a 52 px row, so they paint over the row below. The
column widths sum past 1280 px, so `actions` — the only per-row verb
on the page — lands roughly 388 px off-screen.

**Fix**:

```tsx
<Box sx={{ height: 600, width: '100%' }}>
  <ReportDataGrid
    columns={[
      { field: 'display_name', flex: 1.2, minWidth: 160 },
      { field: 'role', width: 168, renderCell: clampedChipWithTooltip },
      { field: 'evidence_kind', width: 132, renderCell: clampedTextWithTooltip },
      { field: 'top_finding_categories', flex: 1, minWidth: 140, renderCell: clampedTextWithTooltip },
      { field: 'actions', width: 118, disableColumnMenu: true, disableReorder: true },
    ]}
    checkboxSelection
    pinnedColumns={{ left: ['__check__', 'display_name'], right: ['actions'] }}
    sx={{
      '& .MuiDataGrid-cell': {
        overflow: 'hidden',
        display: 'flex',
        alignItems: 'center',
      },
    }}
  />
</Box>
```

Chips become single-line clamped text with `Tooltip` + `aria-label`,
so nothing wraps inside a 52 px row. `actions` is pinned right, so
Rescan is visible at scrollLeft 0 at both 1280 and 1024. The change
is then proved by the bounding-box spec above — not by the build log.

## Where this is currently enforced

| Surface | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `ui/src/pages/CurrentStateCompliancePage.tsx` | remediation in flight — clamped cells + pinned actions landed; bounding-box spec pending |
| `ui/e2e/current-state-compliance-embedded-live.spec.ts` | partial — asserts behavior (D1-D9), not geometry; layout asserts pending |
| `ui/src/components/dataGrid/ReportDataGrid.tsx` | shared wrapper; the right place for a future default `getRowHeight` guard |
| Other admin grids (`UserGroupsPage`, `PamEntitlementsPage`, `SessionRecordingsPage`, `RolesCatalogPanel`, ...) | already on `autoHeight` + `getRowHeight={() => 'auto'}` — compliant by pattern A, unverified by assertion |
| Remaining admin pages | audit ongoing — every page needs one viewport assertion |
| Win32 / Tauri configurator dialogs | inherit the "primary action visible" clause; verified by `scripts/audit-section-508.ps1` plus screenshots |

The "audit ongoing" and "unverified" rows are not necessarily
defective. For acceptance purposes "unverified" is equivalent to
"defective" until a bounding-box assertion or an inspected
screenshot records the verification.

